From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] feat(ssd/l2): async L2 writer thread (decouples writes
 from producer)

NOTE: Structural reference patch, not git-am-ready. Hunks modify
ds4_cuda.cu in three places: the `cuda_stream_load_pool` struct
definition, the worker Phase 2c, and the existing `ds4_gpu_cleanup`
function. Re-locate with grep before applying.



On P2P-capable hardware, the sync write-through from patch 0003 still
costs ~0.28 ms per miss on the producer's critical path (the worker
issues the peer-copy and waits). That's small individually but adds up
to ~9 ms/token across ~31 misses, and it competes with the worker's
SSD reads for the PCIe root complex.

This patch decouples writes by introducing a persistent background
thread that owns L2 writes:

  - struct cuda_stream_load_pool gains:
      std::thread l2_writer_thread
      std::mutex l2_writer_mutex
      std::condition_variable l2_writer_cv
      std::queue<l2_write_job> l2_writer_queue
      std::atomic<bool> l2_writer_exit
      cudaStream_t l2_writer_stream   (on l2_tier)
      std::vector<cudaEvent_t> l2_slot_write_events  (sized to L2 capacity)

  - cuda_stream_l2_write_through_async(job): push to queue, signal cv.
    Replaces the sync call in worker Phase 2c.

  - The writer thread:
      loop:
        wait on cv for non-empty queue
        pop job
        claim L2 slot under l2_mutex
        cudaMemcpyPeerAsync(primary -> l2, l2_writer_stream)
        cudaEventRecord(l2_slot_write_events[l2_slot], l2_writer_stream)
        publish metadata under l2_mutex, set slot.event_index

  - Phase 2a (L2 lookup) gains a sync step:
        if l2_slot.write_event != NULL:
            cudaEventSynchronize(l2_slot.write_event)
        ... then the existing peer-copy-out.

  - Queue depth capped at 64. If full, the worker's _async call becomes
    a no-op (the expert just won't be in L2 next time; the SSD read
    path remains correct).

The thread is created lazily on the first write_through_async call,
and joined at program exit via a new teardown hook in ds4_gpu_cleanup.

On no-P2P hardware: the writer is never started (no _async calls are
made because pool->l2_cache is NULL). No behaviour change.

